<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And, uh, yeah, uh, the largest moth is also in the family Saturnity, and it's the Atlas moth.

726
00:46:44.500 --> 00:46:45.502
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: That's really cool.

727
00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:55.400
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And it's endemic to Asia. And, uh, again, animal crossing.

728
00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:56.400
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Yep.

729
00:46:56.119 --> 00:46:59.002
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: We know all about the Atlas.

730
00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:11.739
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: I always wonder about like the, the species that they chose for animal crossing because I have a friend who's a palaeontologist and sometimes I'll send him like screenshots of my museum and then he'll just tell me about them and I'm like, wow.

731
00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:12.079
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Mm-hmm.

732
00:47:11.739 --> 00:47:12.699
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Cool.

733
00:47:12.559 --> 00:47:19.460
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Some of them make sense, and it is really funny, like, they'll have ones like that where it's like the Atlas Moth, and I'm like, yes, period.

734
00:47:19.460 --> 00:47:22.159
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: That is uh, a moth.

735
00:47:22.159 --> 00:47:25.460
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And then they'll have other ones where it's like, bumblebee.

736
00:47:25.460 --> 00:47:27.800
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And I'm like, okay, there's like a 100 bumblebee species.

737
00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:31.340
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So, like, which one is? Or, like, stag beetle.

738
00:47:31.340 --> 00:47:32.360
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And I'm like, okay.

739
00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:33.019
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Dang Beetle.

740
00:47:33.199 --> 00:47:35.059
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: They try.

741
00:47:35.059 --> 00:47:38.480
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: They try to diversify it a little bit, but.

742
00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:37.940
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: It's just hard.

743
00:47:37.940 --> 00:47:40.400
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, but speaking of Beatles,

744
00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:38.960
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Yeah.

745
00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:42.380
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: We'll move on to the Beatles now.

746
00:47:42.380 --> 00:47:48.019
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So coleoptera is the name of the order, and coleoptera means sheath wing.

747
00:47:48.079 --> 00:47:52.039
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And this, they are so named because they're 4 wings.

748
00:47:52.039 --> 00:47:57.800
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So there's normally 4 pairs of wings, the 4 or like front wings and the hind wings.

749
00:47:57.800 --> 00:48:05.539
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And in beetles, the 4 wings, so the front ones are modified into a sheath, uh, known as the elytra.

750
00:48:05.539 --> 00:48:09.380
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: If you've ever played Minecraft, you know that the elytra are pretty dope.

751
00:48:09.380 --> 00:48:09.980
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: You can fly with them.

752
00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:21.679
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, but basically these provide a hardcover that they can then fold their their hindwings that they used to fly like up under there.

753
00:48:21.679 --> 00:48:29.659
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So some people think that beetles don't even have wings because, I mean, 9 times at a time when you see a beetle, it's like crawling around.

754
00:48:29.659 --> 00:48:36.980
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: But if you bother it enough, it'll, it'll open its, um, elytra and the wings are like spring loaded.

755
00:48:36.980 --> 00:48:40.880
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: They like pop out and then they'll fly and they are not good flyers.

756
00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:41.960
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Beatles suck at flying.

757
00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:42.519
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: No.

758
00:48:42.320 --> 00:48:44.780
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: They're so clumsy.

759
00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:50.739
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Yeah, we've seen a few where like the kids will be looking at it and then it'll just kind of pop up and they're like, what?

760
00:48:50.239 --> 00:48:54.679
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: It can be very, it can be very startling.

761
00:48:50.739 --> 00:48:53.139
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: And then it kind of bumbles away.

762
00:48:55.400 --> 00:48:57.800
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um.

763
00:48:57.800 --> 00:49:12.260
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: This is a quote from uh, Wikipedia, the roughly 400,000 species make up about 40% of all arthropod species so far described, and about 25% of all animals.

764
00:49:12.440 --> 00:49:15.199
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: One in 4 animals.

765
00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:13.502
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Wow.

766
00:49:15.800 --> 00:49:17.840
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Beatles be awesome.

767
00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:21.139
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, So far that we know of.

768
00:49:18.500 --> 00:49:20.000
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: So far that we know of.

769
00:49:21.139 --> 00:49:34.280
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So some papers, uh, some of the more recent papers that are trying to get an estimate of what the true diversity is, have estimated around 1500000 Beetle species, which means that we haven't even found a 3rd of them.

770
00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:32.502
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Wow.

771
00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:38.002
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: We're so...

772
00:49:35.059 --> 00:49:39.679
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: We are not even a 3rd of the way through the Beatles.

773
00:49:39.679 --> 00:49:41.480
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: and the Beatles are everywhere.

774
00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:45.079
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, there's about 170 described families.

775
00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:43.002
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Wow.

776
00:49:45.079 --> 00:50:11.000
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And I just love, uh, there, there was an evolutionary biologist named J.B.S Halding that, uh, when he was looking at, uh, thinking, he was talking about theologians and Christians, and as an evolutionary biologist, he was talking to them and said, well, of, of your god, uh, he must have an inordinate fondness for beetles because there's a heck ton of these.

777
00:50:10.179 --> 00:50:11.920
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Yeah.

778
00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:16.460
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: But the exact quote is an inordinate fondness for beetles.

779
00:50:16.460 --> 00:50:20.179
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And that is just, we just, God was a bug collector.

780
00:50:17.500 --> 00:50:19.000
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: God was a book collector.

781
00:50:20.179 --> 00:50:21.619
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: God loved bugs, you guys.

782
00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:28.940
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So if you're, if you are a true, true Christian, you should probably, uh, love bugs a little bit more.

783
00:50:29.119 --> 00:50:31.400
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Not fear them as much.

784
00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:31.559
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Oh, you're a true Christian.

785
00:50:31.559 --> 00:50:33.502
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: What are your 10 favourite beetles?

786
00:50:33.500 --> 00:50:40.460
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Oh my god, I almost, I was taking a drink of water and I almost spit it across.

787
00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:43.219
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Okay.

788
00:50:43.219 --> 00:50:47.480
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, let me see where I want to do.

789
00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:47.780
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Okay.

790
00:50:47.780 --> 00:50:55.219
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: The, um, of Beatles, the most diverse Beatle family are the rove beetles, uh, Staphylinity.

791
00:50:55.219 --> 00:50:56.000
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: These.

792
00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:58.340
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: I forgot to make notes for this, so I'm just gonna riff.

793
00:50:58.340 --> 00:51:03.559
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, these Beatles are often, uh, detritivores.

794
00:51:03.559 --> 00:51:07.159
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: They often will eat like things that are breaking down and decaying.

795
00:51:07.159 --> 00:51:12.920
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, and they just, they have so many different shapes and sizes.

796
00:51:12.920 --> 00:51:19.820
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: There are so many families or things that we thought were an entirely different family, that it's like, mm, nope, it's a rope beetle, actually.

797
00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:21.679
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um,

798
00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:39.920
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: We make jokes in the museum that I've worked at and then also just museums, uh, elsewhere that people, I know, some of my friends, where if you can't, if you're trying to identify a beetle, if you're going through the dichotomous key and trying to figure out which beetle it is and you're just stumped, it's probably a rove beetle.

799
00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:42.739
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: It's probably just a really weird one.

800
00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:42.820
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Just throw it over there.

801
00:51:42.739 --> 00:51:54.380
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Because, like, the thing about Rove Beatles is that the one of they don't really have a quote unquote defining characteristic because the what used to be considered the defining characteristic is that they're Elytra are incredibly short.

802
00:51:54.380 --> 00:52:09.679
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So they basically have, like, for most beetles, when you think of a beetle, you don't see any of the abdomen, because the entire abdomen is covered with that sheath, that, um, they're elytra, but staphylinids have a really shortened elytra.

803
00:52:09.679 --> 00:52:15.079
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So some of their abdominal segments are exposed, usually up to 4 of them, 4 or more of them.

804
00:52:15.500 --> 00:52:22.760
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: But then, of course, because there's always an exception, people found rove beetles that don't have that necessarily.

805
00:52:22.760 --> 00:52:29.900
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: There are some that are like, and they're usually like flat and thin, and then of course we found some that are like chonky.

806
00:52:29.900 --> 00:52:33.260
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, oh, they normally are pretty sleek.

807
00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:37.820
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Nope, we found some that are really like ribbed or ridged.

808
00:52:37.820 --> 00:52:39.320
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: like they have like a lot of,

809
00:52:38.500 --> 00:52:39.760
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Out into the church.

810
00:52:39.320 --> 00:52:41.599
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Yeah, it's like, nope, they did that too.

811
00:52:42.019 --> 00:52:52.880
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And we basically, you know, from, from just looking at them morphologically and and trying to figure out what they are, just how they look, they might seem like a different species.

812
00:52:52.880 --> 00:52:54.920
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: But then looking at the DNA.

813
00:52:54.920 --> 00:52:59.239
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: We realise, oh, no, they're still, they're still within, within this family.

814
00:52:59.239 --> 00:53:03.619
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, So this family might get broken up eventually.

815
00:53:03.619 --> 00:53:08.360
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: We don't know, or it might or we might, it might continue to subsume all of the other Beatles.

816
00:53:08.360 --> 00:53:10.699
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, we don't know.

817
00:53:10.699 --> 00:53:11.480
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: But they're really cool.

818
00:53:11.480 --> 00:53:19.159
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, and then I just had to shout out my favourite Beatle family.

819
00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:23.420
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: These are, so it's the family cryptofagedy.

820
00:53:23.420 --> 00:53:28.760
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Uh, that name means feeding in hidden places because for a long time.

821
00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:30.440
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: They really didn't know what they ate.

822
00:53:30.440 --> 00:53:31.579
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So they were like, I don't know.

823
00:53:31.579 --> 00:53:32.360
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: I don't know what they eat.

824
00:53:32.360 --> 00:53:34.099
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So let's just call them.

825
00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:34.980
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Something is sustaining them somewhere.

826
00:53:34.099 --> 00:53:36.679
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Yeah, I don't know what they eat.

827
00:53:36.679 --> 00:53:40.519
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So let's just call them unknown eating, like crypto fagid.

828
00:53:40.519 --> 00:53:42.559
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: That's just what we're going to call them.

829
00:53:42.559 --> 00:54:02.480
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, but they are, uh, both the adults and the larvae appear to feed exclusively on fungus, but the fungus can be in some really weird situations, like, the fungus that grows on animal fur that has been shed or bird feathers that have been shed.

830
00:54:02.480 --> 00:54:06.860
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, And then some also do like rotting wood, whatever.

831
00:54:06.860 --> 00:54:11.239
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And so that has given them their common name of silken fungus beetles.

832
00:54:11.239 --> 00:54:17.780
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And they're called silken because they have they have a good amount of usually hair, like really fine hair all over their body.

833
00:54:17.780 --> 00:54:24.019
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: And they also are just this, they're usually this little, um, like, light caramel colour.

834
00:54:24.019 --> 00:54:24.559
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: They really cute.

835
00:54:24.559 --> 00:54:27.559
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: But the big thing.

836
00:54:27.559 --> 00:54:33.320
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: If you're not sold, I have to refer you to the genus Antherophagus, which means flower eating.

837
00:54:33.320 --> 00:54:37.820
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: These are known as the bumblebee riding beetles.

838
00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:39.820
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: Adorable.

839
00:54:39.139 --> 00:54:47.059
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So I have, I have put a picture of this in the show notes.

840
00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:41.980
<v 8497A15A-7A11-458A-8F16-F3D257CDAA83>Chelsea: This photo needs to be everywhere.

841
00:54:47.059 --> 00:54:50.059
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: So either go look at it or just look up this genus.

842
00:54:50.059 --> 00:55:01.699
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: Um, or just look up Bumblebee Riding beetles, uh, specifically look it up in Bugguide.net, which is the website, uh, that a lot of us, that a lot of North American entomologists use.

843
00:55:01.699 --> 00:55:05.539
<v 0E8DEE05-ABEA-4C86-AD98-8916E3074EBA>Codey: There's a lot of really good photos on there of these things.

